# /projects/ has no page of its own — people truncate URLs, so send it to `/`,
# which IS the Project index (docs/content-architecture.md §1).
#
# THESE ARE EXACT PATHS AND MUST STAY EXACT. This was `/projects/* / 301`, and
# the splat did not just match the truncated URL — it matched every Project
# page underneath it. All six 301'd to `/`, so the section was unreachable in
# production and every link on the index bounced straight back to the index.
#
# IT WAS INVISIBLE TO EVERY LOCAL CHECK, and that is the part worth remembering:
# this file is a Cloudflare edge feature, so `astro dev`, `astro preview` and
# anything driving them serve the Project pages correctly. Only the deployed
# site redirects. The gate that did catch it is `npm run check:served`, which
# runs after deploy and compares the Served Document against the build — it
# reported the six pages as serving `/`'s document, and it had been failing on
# `main` since the routes landed. Do not add a wildcard back here without
# reading that gate's output on the next deploy.
#
# This is here rather than in Astro's `redirects` config, and that contradicts
# what §1 originally said. The reason is that a static Astro build cannot emit a
# real 301: it writes an HTML page carrying <meta http-equiv="refresh">
# (node_modules/astro/dist/core/routing/3xx.js). That is a 200 with a client-side
# hop, it costs a round trip, and — because @astrojs/sitemap's filter excludes
# only /404/ — it would be listed in the sitemap as a canonical URL.
#
# Cloudflare's static assets serve _redirects natively from the asset directory,
# so this is a real edge 301 at zero bytes. public/ is copied verbatim into dist/,
# which is exactly what this file needs and the reason it cannot live in src/.
#
# Both spellings, because the truncation this exists for is a person deleting
# from the end of a URL and they do not reliably stop on the slash. Without the
# second line `/projects` served the 404 page, which is a worse answer to the
# same question: `html_handling: "auto-trailing-slash"` only normalises a path
# that resolves to an asset, and there is no asset here to resolve to.
/projects/ / 301
/projects / 301
